{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>Please, El, please heal her.</b> Scripture comes to teach you derech eretz: one who asks something of his fellow must say two or three words of supplication — and only then may he make his requests (Sifrei)." "<b>Saying.</b> What does תַּ\"ל? He said to Him: Answer me — are You healing her or not? — until He answered him: ״וְאָבִיהָ יָרֹק יָרַק״ and so forth. Rabbi Yishmael says: In four places Moses asked before the Holy One, blessed be He, to answer him whether He would fulfill his requests or not. Of the same sort: ״וַיְדַבֵּר מֹשֶׁה לִפְנֵי יהוה לֵאמֹר״ and so forth (Exodus 6) — what does תַּ\"ל ״לֵאמֹר״? Answer me — are You their redeemer or not? — until He answered him: ״עַתָּה תִרְאֶה״ and so forth. Of the same sort: ״וַיְדַבֵּר מֹשֶׁה לִפְנֵי יהוה לֵאמֹר יִפְקֹד יהוה אֱלֹהֵי הָרוּחֹת לְכָל בָּשָׂר״ (Numbers 27) — He answered him: ״קַח לְךָ״. Of the same sort: ״וָאֶתְחַנַּן אֶל יהוה בָּעֵת הַהִוא לֵאמֹר״ (Deuteronomy 3) — He answered him: ״רַב לָךְ״ (Sifrei)." "<b>Please heal her.</b> For what reason did Moses not prolong his prayer? So that Israel not say: His sister is standing in distress and he stands and prolongs his prayer (Sifrei). Another interpretation: so that Israel not say: For his sister he prolongs his prayer, but for us he does not prolong his prayer."], :book :numbers, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:10:41.371298403Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}